==English==

===Etymology===
{{ety|en|:af|direct|-ly<id:adverbial>|text=+|tree=1}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|dĭ-rĕk(t)ˈ -lē|dī-rĕk(t)ˈ -lē|a=RP,GA,CA}}, {{IPA|en|/dɪˈɹɛk(t).li/|/daɪˈɹɛk(t).li/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-directly.ogg|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-ca-directly.ogg|a=CA}}
* {{IPA|en|/dɪˈɹek(t).li/|/dɑeˈɹek(t).li/|a=AU}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv}}

# In a [[direct]] manner; in a [[straight]] line or course.
#: {{ux|en|He drove '''directly''' to the office, and didn't stop off at the petrol station.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1855|author=William Wells Brown|title=Sketches of Places and People Abroad|page=255
|passage=On arriving at the doors, and entering a long, capacious passage, our eyes became quite dazzled by the gleams of colored light which shone upon them, both '''directly''' and reflectedly.}}
# In a [[straightforward]] way; without anything [[intervene|intervening]]; not by [[secondary]] but by direct means.
#: {{ux|en|I'm sick of asking you to fire him; I'll just do it '''directly'''.}}
# [[plainly|Plainly]], without [[circumlocution]] or [[ambiguity]]; [[absolutely]]; in [[express]] terms.
#: {{ux|en|I'm going to tell Natalie '''directly''' that I love her.}}
#: {{ux|en|To put it more '''directly''': he's not 'made redundant' but sacked.}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|date=April 19, 2012|author=Josh Halliday
|title=Free speech haven or lawless cesspool – can the internet be civilised?
|url=http://www.guardian.co.uk/technology/2012/apr/19/free-speech-haven-lawless-cesspool
|passage="Mujtahidd" has attracted almost 300,000 followers since the end of last year, when he began posting scandalous claims about the Saudi elite. In one tweet, Mujtahidd '''directly''' challenged Prince Abdul Aziz Bin Fahd about his political history: "Did you resign or were you forced to resign from your post as head of the diwan [office] of the council of ministers?"}}
# [[exactly|Exactly]]; [[just]]; at the shortest possible distance.
#: {{ux|en|It's '''directly''' across the street.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2013|month=September-October|author=w:Henry Petroski
|magazine=w:American Scientist|title=[http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/pub/2013/5/the-evolution-of-eyeglasses The Evolution of Eyeglasses]
|passage=The ability of a segment of a glass sphere to magnify whatever is placed before it was known around the year 1000, when the spherical segment was called a reading stone,{{...}}. Scribes, illuminators, and scholars held such stones '''directly''' over manuscript pages as an aid in seeing what was being written, drawn, or read.}}
# [[straightforwardly|Straightforwardly]]; [[honestly]].
#: {{ux|en|He told me '''directly''' that he'd cheated on me, and how sorry he was for it.}}
# {{lb|en|dated}} [[immediately|Immediately]].
# {{lb|en|chiefly|dated|or|dialect|including|South Midlands|Cornwall|Southern US}} [[soon|Soon]]; [[next#Adverb|next]]; [[in due time]]; as soon as it becomes [[convenient]].
#: {{ux|en|We'll go to the store '''directly''', but first I need to finish sweeping.}}

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|in a straight line or course}} {{l|en|straightwise}}, {{l|en|undeviatingly}}
* {{sense|without circumlocution or ambiguity}} {{l|en|bluntly}}, {{l|en|clearly}}, {{l|en|downrightly}}, {{l|en|unambiguously}}; see also [[Thesaurus:explicitly]]
* {{sense|exactly|just}} {{l|en|accurately}}, {{l|en|dead#Adverb}}, {{l|en|precisely}}, {{l|en|slap bang}}; see also [[Thesaurus:exactly]]
* {{sense|straightforwardly|honestly}} {{l|en|frankly}}, {{l|en|truthfully}}
* {{sense|immediately}} {{l|en|forthwith}}, {{l|en|now}}, {{l|en|tout de suite}}; see also [[Thesaurus:immediately]]
* {{sense|soon|next}} {{l|en|betimes}}, {{l|en|in due course}}, {{l|en|presently}}, {{l|en|shortly}}; see also [[Thesaurus:soon]]

